-- title:
--   Integrality of X⁶j⁴(j-1728)³ bounds orders of hΔ by gE₄²E₆
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, $k$ an integer, and let $g,h$ be modular forms of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_0(N)$. Let $X$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying $X\cdot\tilde h=\tilde g$ in $\mathbb{C}((q))$, where $\tilde g,\tilde h$ denote the $q$-expansions of $g,h$ at $\infty$ taken with period $1$ and viewed as Laurent series. Write $J=$ [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb ℂ ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) for the image in $\mathbb{C}((q))$ of the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot(\mathrm{jNumQ})$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ under the coefficientwise ring map induced by $\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{C}$, i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$. Assume that $X^6J^4(J-1728)^3$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated by $J$. Then for every $\tau$ in the upper half plane, the analytic order of vanishing at $\tau\in\mathbb{C}$ of the function $z\mapsto h(z)\Delta(z)$, composed with `UpperHalfPlane.ofComplex`, is at most the analytic order of vanishing at $\tau$ of $z\mapsto g(z)E_4(z)^2E_6(z)$, composed with `UpperHalfPlane.ofComplex` (orders taken in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$).
--
--   The statement converts an integrality hypothesis for the quotient $X=\tilde g/\tilde h$ over $\mathbb{C}[j]$ into a pointwise comparison of vanishing orders on the upper half plane, using $j=E_4^3/\Delta$ and $j-1728=E_6^2/\Delta$. It is the divisor-theoretic input to [`ModularCurve.exists_cuspForm_qExpansion_eq_mul_thetaL_of_isIntegral`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_cuspForm_qExpansion_eq_mul_thetaL_of_isIntegral), where the resulting divisibility produces a cusp form with prescribed $q$-expansion.
